{
  "dom_id": "step-e7964",
  "name": "Example: Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form",
  "problem": "What system of inequalities defines the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/assets/e92ea6fc292a771333cc12b9554b58bd4ba5f358195982c5ac3fd6bd2a9d06ff.png)",
  "worked_solution": "Consider the line $y=1.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is \n\n$$\ny < 1.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $x - y = 2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny =x- 2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny \\geq  x - 2,\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx-y \\leq 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y<1 \\\\ x-y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
